WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.510
- [Instructor] What we're going
to do in this video is use

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.620
what we know about polynomials
and how to manipulate them

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.930
and what we've talked about
of whether two polynomials

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.220
are equal to each other for all values

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.220
of the variable that they're written in,

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.080
so whether we're dealing
with a polynomial identity.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.940
And we're going to use
those skills in order

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:21.730
to prove some properties of
relationships between numbers.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.420
So if I were to list out some integers,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:29.130
I could go zero, I could
go one, I could go two,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.080
three, four, five.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.000
And if I were to list
out the squares of these,

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.350
if I were to create a
sequence of integer squares,

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.970
well, zero squared would be zero,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.240
one squared would be one,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.760
two squared is four,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.040
three squared is nine,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.100
four squared is 16,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.490
five squared is 25.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.510
And we could, of course,
keep going in either case.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:55.930
But the first thing I
want you to think about,

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:57.720
before you even write down a polynomial

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.080
or try to construct one,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.460
is look at this sequence
of integer squares.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.280
And do you see any pattern

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.500
in terms of the difference
between successive terms

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.993
of this sequence of integer squares?

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.020
All right, now let's
think about this together.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.180
So to go from zero to one, you add one.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.930
And to go from one to four, you add three.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.940
To go from four to nine,

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.660
you add five.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.360
To go from nine to 16, you add seven.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.020
It seems like a pattern here.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:34.880
As we go to successive terms

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.990
of this sequence of integer squares,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.340
we're adding increasing odd numbers.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.720
So I'm guessing that if I add nine here,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.880
which is the next odd
number, I'm gonna get to 25,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.890
and that indeed is the case.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.210
And you could test that out.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.670
Well, what, if I add 11,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.690
which would be the next odd
number, what do I get to?

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:55.550
I get to 36, which is the square of six.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.650
But how can we feel good
that this always is true,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.150
that this never breaks down?

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.800
Well, one way to do it is

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.500
to think a little bit more generally,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.830
and that's where our
algebra and our knowledge

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:08.760
of polynomials are going to be useful.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.360
So let's say we go all the way,

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:11.700
and we're just speaking generally now.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.520
So we have the number n, and
then with the next number

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.120
after that is going to be n plus one.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.390
And then if we think about
what the corresponding terms

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.510
in the sequence of
integer squares would be,

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.440
well, that would be, when we square it,

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.600
when we get to n, we would get n squared.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.470
And when we get to n plus one,

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.340
we would have n plus one squared.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.350
And let's see if we could
think about what the difference

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.370
between these two things are.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.628
The difference between 25 and 16 is nine.

00:02:38.628 --> 00:02:40.780
Difference between 16 and nine is seven.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:41.970
So let's think about what the difference

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.690
between n plus one
squared and n squared is.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.400
And how do we write that as a polynomial?

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.490
Well, it'll just be n plus one squared

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.150
minus n squared.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.590
And now let's see if we can rewrite this,

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.480
algebraically manipulate
this so we can set up

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.960
a polynomial identity that
describes this pattern

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.470
that we just saw.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.399
So what I'll do is I'm
just going to expand out

00:03:04.399 --> 00:03:07.640
n plus one squared right over there.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.340
So that is going to be n
squared plus two n plus one.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.540
And then we have this
minus n squared here,

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.440
so minus n squared.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.440
And so we see that n squared
minus n squared cancels out.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:25.750
And so we can rewrite
everything we have here as

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:28.370
n plus one squared

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:29.830
minus n squared.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:32.480
So this is really the difference
between successive terms

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.620
in our sequence of integer squares

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.760
is going to be equal to two n plus one

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.870
for any integer n.

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:44.970
Well, for any integer n, what
is two n plus one going to be?

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:45.820
And especially here,

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.330
we're dealing with the positive integers.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:53.560
Well, for any integer n, this
is going to be an odd integer.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.330
If you take any integer,
you multiply it by two,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.380
this part is going to be even.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:00.480
But then you add one to that,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:02.360
you're going to get an odd integer.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:03.920
And you can also see

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.140
that this increases by two as n increases.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.770
So when you go from one odd integer,

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.330
you go add two to the next odd integer.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.220
You add two to the next odd integer,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.270
which is exactly what is described there.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:19.000
So this is pretty neat.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.730
We've just used a little bit of algebra,

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.270
a little bit of what we know
about polynomial identities

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.720
to show that the difference
between successive terms

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.010
in this sequence of integer
squares right over here

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.983
is going to be increasing odd numbers.

